// Detailed notes: concept id (or "term:<Term>") -> { body, example, tip }.
// Modules: DevOps & Automation, System Design & Reliability, Kubernetes & Container Orchestration,
// CI/CD & GitOps, Kubernetes & GitOps, Site Reliability Engineering, Infrastructure as Code Deep Dive.
const n = (body, example, tip) => ({ body, example, tip });

export default {
  // ---------- Shared terms ----------
  'term:Infrastructure as Code': n(
    "Infrastructure as Code (IaC) defines servers, networks, databases, load balancers, IAM policies, and other resources in machine-readable files rather than creating them by hand in a console. Declarative tools such as Terraform and OpenTofu, AWS CloudFormation and CDK, Azure Bicep, and Pulumi describe the desired end state and work out the changes needed; configuration management tools such as Ansible, Chef, and Puppet configure what runs on the servers.\n\nBecause infrastructure lives in version control, every change is reviewed in a pull request, tested, and applied through the same CI/CD pipelines as application code. Environments can be recreated identically (dev matching production), drift from the defined state can be detected, and disasters can be recovered by re-applying code. Security and compliance checks (policy as code, IaC scanners) run before anything is deployed.",
    "A team rebuilds its entire staging environment (VPC, subnets, EKS cluster, and databases) from Terraform in 20 minutes after it is accidentally deleted.",
    "Treat IaC like application code: code review, automated tests and scans, and no manual console changes in production."
  ),
  'term:GitOps': n(
    "GitOps is an operating model in which Git holds the declarative desired state of applications and infrastructure, and an automated agent continuously reconciles the live environment to match it. Changes happen only through commits and pull requests, which are reviewed, tested, and auditable. The agent, such as Argo CD or Flux for Kubernetes, pulls changes from Git rather than having CI push credentials into production.\n\nKey benefits include a complete audit trail of who changed what and when, easy rollback with git revert, automatic correction of manual drift (someone's kubectl edit is reverted), consistent multi-cluster deployment, and a smaller attack surface because CI pipelines do not need direct production credentials. The four OpenGitOps principles are: declarative, versioned and immutable, pulled automatically, and continuously reconciled.",
    "A developer merges a pull request that bumps an image tag; Argo CD notices within a minute and rolls the new version out to three clusters, with no one running kubectl.",
    "In GitOps, protect the Git repository like production: branch protection, required reviews, and signed commits."
  ),

  // ---------- DevOps & Automation ----------
  'd1-1': n(
    "Git is a distributed version control system that records the complete history of a codebase as a series of commits, each a snapshot with an author, timestamp, and message. Every developer has a full copy of the repository, so work continues offline and history is never held in one place.\n\nBranches let people work on features and fixes in isolation, then merge them back, usually through pull (merge) requests on platforms such as GitHub, GitLab, or Bitbucket, where teammates review the code and CI runs automated checks. Common workflows include trunk-based development (short-lived branches merged into main frequently) and GitFlow (long-lived develop and release branches). Git makes it possible to find when a bug was introduced (git bisect), roll back safely, and collaborate at scale. Protecting the main branch and scanning for committed secrets are essential security practices.",
    "A bug appears in production; `git log` and `git blame` show which commit changed the code, and `git revert` safely undoes it within minutes.",
    "Anything committed stays in history. Rotate secrets that were ever committed, even if later deleted."
  ),
  'd1-2': n(
    "Continuous Integration is the practice of merging developers' changes into a shared main branch frequently, at least daily, with every push automatically triggering a build and a test suite. Problems surface within minutes of being introduced, while the change is small and the context fresh, instead of weeks later during a painful 'integration phase'.\n\nA healthy CI setup has fast, reliable tests (unit tests first, then integration), static analysis and linting, security scans (SAST, dependency, and secret scanning), and a rule that a failing build blocks merging and gets fixed immediately. Short-lived branches or trunk-based development plus feature flags keep changes small. The result is a main branch that is always in a working, releasable state.",
    "A pull request fails CI because a unit test catches an off-by-one error in pricing logic; it is fixed before merge, so no customer ever sees the bug.",
    "Keep the CI pipeline fast (ideally under 10 minutes). Slow pipelines encourage big batches and skipped tests."
  ),
  'd1-3': n(
    "Continuous Delivery extends CI so that every change that passes the pipeline is automatically built into a deployable artifact, tested in production-like environments, and ready to release to production at any time, usually with a one-click, human-approved deployment. Continuous Deployment goes one step further: every change that passes all automated checks is released to production automatically, with no manual gate.\n\nBoth depend on thorough automated testing, repeatable deployments, infrastructure as code, and good monitoring. They make releases small, frequent, and boring, which reduces risk because each change is easy to understand and roll back. Safe release strategies (canary, blue/green, feature flags) and automated rollback on bad metrics make high deployment frequency practical.",
    "An online retailer deploys to production 50 times a day with continuous deployment; each change is small, and a canary automatically rolls back any release that raises errors.",
    "Delivery = always releasable, manual trigger. Deployment = automatic release to production."
  ),
  'd1-4': n(
    "A CI/CD pipeline is the automated sequence of stages that takes code from commit to production: checkout, build, unit tests, static analysis and security scans, packaging an immutable artifact (a container image or package), deploying to test or staging environments, integration and end-to-end tests, approval (if required), production deployment, and post-deployment verification.\n\nEach stage acts as a quality gate: a failure stops the change from progressing. Pipelines are defined as code (YAML in GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, Azure Pipelines, or Jenkinsfiles), stored in the repository, reviewed, and versioned like application code. Build once and promote the same artifact through environments, so what was tested is exactly what ships. Pipelines hold powerful credentials, so they must be secured.",
    "A GitHub Actions workflow runs tests, builds a Docker image, scans it with Trivy, pushes it to ECR, and deploys it to staging; a manual approval promotes the same image digest to production.",
    "Build the artifact once and promote it. Rebuilding for each environment means production runs something you never tested."
  ),
  'd1-6': n(
    "Automated tests give confidence to change code quickly. The test pyramid guides the mix: many fast unit tests at the base (individual functions and classes in isolation, running in milliseconds), fewer integration tests in the middle (components working together with real databases, queues, or APIs), and a small number of slow end-to-end tests at the top (full user journeys through the UI or API).\n\nOther useful types include contract tests (verifying API agreements between services), smoke tests (quick checks after deployment), performance and load tests, and security tests (SAST, DAST, and dependency scanning). All run automatically in the pipeline and block releases on failure. Tests must be reliable, because flaky tests erode trust and get ignored. Coverage is a guide, not a goal: test the behaviour and risks that matter.",
    "A payment service has 800 unit tests (2 seconds), 60 integration tests against a test database (1 minute), and 5 end-to-end checkout tests (3 minutes), all run on every merge.",
    "Fix or quarantine flaky tests immediately. A test suite nobody trusts is worse than no tests."
  ),
  'd1-7': n(
    "Monitoring and observability give teams visibility into how systems behave in production. Monitoring collects metrics (CPU, memory, request rate, error rate, latency), logs, and health checks, and alerts when something crosses a threshold or an SLO starts burning. Tools include AWS CloudWatch, Azure Monitor, Google Cloud Monitoring, Prometheus with Grafana, Datadog, and New Relic.\n\nObservability goes further, adding distributed traces and rich, high-cardinality telemetry so engineers can ask new questions about unexpected failures without shipping new code. In DevOps, observability closes the feedback loop: every deployment is watched for regressions, automated rollback reacts to bad metrics, and production data informs development priorities. Good alerts are actionable and tied to runbooks.",
    "After a deployment, dashboards show error rates rising for one endpoint; traces identify the new code path, and the team rolls back within minutes.",
    "You cannot safely deploy often without good observability. Invest in it before increasing deployment frequency."
  ),

  // ---------- System Design & Reliability ----------
  'a1-1': n(
    "A distributed system is a collection of independent computers that communicate over a network and coordinate to appear as a single system to users. Distribution delivers horizontal scalability, fault tolerance through redundancy, and low latency by placing nodes near users.\n\nIt also introduces fundamental difficulties: partial failures (some nodes or links fail while others keep working), unreliable networks with variable latency and dropped or duplicated messages, the lack of a global clock (making ordering hard), consistency trade-offs (the CAP theorem), and concurrency issues. The 'fallacies of distributed computing', such as 'the network is reliable' and 'latency is zero', describe assumptions that break real systems. Designing for failure (timeouts, retries with backoff, idempotency, replication, consensus algorithms such as Raft, and graceful degradation) is the core skill.",
    "An online store's order service times out calling payments; without idempotency keys, a retry could charge the customer twice.",
    "Assume every network call can fail, be slow, or happen twice, and design for each case."
  ),
  'a1-2': n(
    "Microservices architecture structures an application as a set of small services, each aligned to a business capability (bounded context), independently deployable, owning its own data, and communicating through well-defined APIs or events. Teams can own services end to end, deploy them independently, choose suitable technologies, and scale hot services without scaling everything.\n\nThe trade-offs are significant: network calls replace in-process calls (latency and failures), data consistency spans services (sagas and eventual consistency instead of ACID transactions), and operations require service discovery, API gateways, centralised logging, distributed tracing, and mature CI/CD. Organisational fit matters (Conway's law: systems mirror team communication structures). Many teams do better with a modular monolith until scale or team size clearly justifies splitting.",
    "A streaming company runs hundreds of microservices so the recommendations team can deploy several times a day without coordinating with the billing team.",
    "Do not split into microservices to fix messy code. A distributed mess is harder to fix than a monolithic one."
  ),
  'a1-3': n(
    "Event-driven systems communicate by publishing events (facts about something that happened, such as 'PaymentReceived') to a broker or event bus, and letting any interested services consume them asynchronously. Common infrastructure includes Apache Kafka, RabbitMQ, Amazon EventBridge, SNS and SQS, Azure Service Bus, and Google Pub/Sub.\n\nBenefits include loose coupling (producers do not know or wait for consumers), independent scaling, buffering of traffic spikes, easy addition of new consumers, and resilience when downstream services are temporarily unavailable. Patterns include publish/subscribe, event sourcing (storing state as a sequence of events), and CQRS. Challenges include eventual consistency, debugging flows across services, message ordering, duplicate delivery (consumers must be idempotent), schema evolution, and handling failed messages with dead-letter queues.",
    "When a user signs up, one 'UserRegistered' event triggers the welcome email, CRM sync, analytics, and fraud-screening services independently.",
    "Design consumers to be idempotent and monitor dead-letter queues. Unprocessed events are silent data loss."
  ),
  'a1-4': n(
    "An API is a contract between systems, and good design keeps it clear, consistent, and stable. REST APIs model resources with nouns and HTTP methods (GET /orders/123, POST /orders), use proper status codes, support pagination, filtering, and sorting, return consistent error formats, and are documented with OpenAPI. gRPC uses Protocol Buffers over HTTP/2 for efficient, strongly typed, streaming-capable communication, which suits internal service-to-service calls. GraphQL lets clients request exactly the data they need.\n\nVersion APIs (for example /v2/ or header-based versioning) and evolve them backward-compatibly, adding fields rather than removing or renaming them, so existing clients do not break. Make operations idempotent where retries are likely (idempotency keys for POSTs). Secure APIs with authentication (OAuth 2.0 and OIDC), per-request authorisation, rate limiting, and input validation, following the OWASP API Security Top 10.",
    "A payments API accepts an Idempotency-Key header, so a mobile app retrying a timed-out 'charge' request never creates a duplicate payment.",
    "Broken object-level authorisation (users accessing others' objects by ID) is the top API risk. Check authorisation for every object."
  ),
  'term:Observability': n(
    "Observability is the ability to understand what is happening inside a system by examining its outputs, well enough to explain behaviour you did not predict. It rests on three main signal types. Logs are timestamped records of discrete events with rich context. Metrics are numeric time series (latency, error rate, throughput, saturation) that are cheap to store and ideal for dashboards and alerts. Traces follow individual requests across services, revealing where time is spent and where failures originate.\n\nIn distributed systems, observability is essential because failures emerge from interactions between many components. Correlating the signals (trace IDs in logs, deployment markers on metrics), instrumenting with OpenTelemetry, and emitting high-cardinality context (version, region, customer tier) enable fast diagnosis. Some add profiles and events as further signals.",
    "An alert on rising error rates leads to a trace showing failures in the inventory service, whose logs reveal a new database migration locking a table.",
    "Instrument before you need it. You cannot debug last night's outage with telemetry you were not collecting."
  ),
  'a1-6': n(
    "Service Level Indicators (SLIs) are carefully chosen measurements of service behaviour that users care about: availability (the ratio of successful requests), latency (the proportion of requests faster than a threshold), throughput, durability, or freshness. Service Level Objectives (SLOs) are internal targets for SLIs over a window, for example 99.9% of requests succeed and 95% complete within 300 ms over 30 days. Service Level Agreements (SLAs) are contractual commitments to customers, with penalties such as service credits, and are normally set looser than the SLOs.\n\nThe error budget (100% minus the SLO) quantifies acceptable unreliability. Teams spend it on releases and experiments; when it is exhausted, they prioritise reliability work over features. This aligns product and engineering on a shared, measurable definition of 'reliable enough', because 100% is neither achievable nor worth the cost.",
    "A 99.9% availability SLO over 30 days allows about 43 minutes of errors; an SLA promising 99.5% gives the provider margin before credits are owed.",
    "Define SLIs from the user's perspective, measured as close to the user as possible, not just server CPU."
  ),
  'a1-7': n(
    "Chaos engineering is the discipline of experimenting on a system to build confidence that it can withstand turbulent real-world conditions. Teams deliberately inject failures, such as terminating instances or containers, adding network latency or packet loss, exhausting CPU, memory, or disk, failing dependencies, or simulating an Availability Zone outage, and verify that the system keeps meeting its steady-state behaviour (for example SLOs).\n\nExperiments follow the scientific method: define the steady state, form a hypothesis, limit the blast radius, run the experiment with an abort mechanism, and learn from the results. Netflix pioneered the practice with Chaos Monkey and the Simian Army; modern tools include AWS Fault Injection Service, Gremlin, Chaos Mesh, and LitmusChaos. Findings, such as missing timeouts, single points of failure, and untested failover, are fixed before a real outage exposes them.",
    "An experiment adding 500 ms of latency to the payment dependency shows checkout threads piling up; adding a timeout and a circuit breaker fixes a hidden cascading-failure risk.",
    "Start in staging with small blast radii and clear abort criteria before running chaos experiments in production."
  ),
  'a1-8': n(
    "Scalability patterns target specific bottlenecks. Caching (in-memory stores such as Redis or Memcached, CDNs, HTTP caching) serves repeated reads without hitting the database or origin. Horizontal scaling adds stateless instances behind load balancers. Read replicas offload read traffic from a primary database. Sharding partitions data across multiple databases by a key to scale writes and storage.\n\nAsynchronous processing with queues (SQS, Kafka, RabbitMQ) decouples producers from consumers, absorbs spikes, and lets work proceed at a sustainable rate. Other tools include connection pooling, rate limiting and back-pressure, denormalisation and precomputation, and CQRS. The key is to measure first, find the real constraint (CPU, database connections, I/O, or a hot key), and apply the pattern that addresses it, since each adds complexity.",
    "A news site survives a viral story by serving pages from a CDN cache and moving comment processing onto a queue, instead of adding database servers.",
    "Measure before optimising. Scaling the wrong tier wastes money and leaves the real bottleneck in place."
  ),

  // ---------- Kubernetes & Container Orchestration ----------
  'k8-3': n(
    "A Deployment is a Kubernetes object that declares the desired state for a stateless application: which container image to run, how many replicas, resource requests and limits, environment configuration, and the update strategy. The Deployment controller manages ReplicaSets, which in turn keep the requested number of identical Pods running, recreating Pods that crash or are evicted.\n\nWhen you change the image or configuration, the Deployment performs a rolling update by default: it gradually creates new Pods and removes old ones, respecting maxSurge and maxUnavailable settings and waiting for readiness probes, so the service stays available. kubectl rollout status shows progress, kubectl rollout history lists revisions, and kubectl rollout undo returns to the previous version. Stateful workloads use StatefulSets instead.",
    "Updating a Deployment's image from v1.4 to v1.5 replaces Pods two at a time; when v1.5 fails its readiness checks, the rollout halts and `kubectl rollout undo` restores v1.4.",
    "Always set readiness probes. Without them, rolling updates send traffic to Pods that are not ready yet."
  ),
  'k8-4': n(
    "Pods are ephemeral and get new IP addresses when they are recreated, so Kubernetes Services provide a stable virtual IP and DNS name that load-balances across the healthy Pods matching a label selector. Cluster DNS (CoreDNS) resolves names such as orders.shop.svc.cluster.local, so applications call each other by name.\n\nService types: ClusterIP (the default; reachable only inside the cluster), NodePort (opens the same port on every node for external access), LoadBalancer (asks the cloud provider to create an external load balancer), and ExternalName (a DNS alias to an outside name). Headless Services (clusterIP: None) skip the virtual IP and return individual Pod IPs, which StatefulSets use for stable per-Pod identities. EndpointSlices track which Pod IPs currently back each Service.",
    "The frontend calls http://cart:8080; as cart Pods scale from 3 to 12 and restart, the Service keeps routing to whichever Pods are ready.",
    "Only expose Services externally when needed. Prefer ClusterIP plus an Ingress or Gateway with TLS and authentication."
  ),
  'k8-5': n(
    "An Ingress defines HTTP and HTTPS routing rules from outside the cluster to internal Services, based on host names and URL paths, for example api.example.com/orders to the orders Service and www.example.com to the web Service. It typically terminates TLS using certificates stored as Secrets, often issued automatically by cert-manager with Let's Encrypt.\n\nAn Ingress resource does nothing by itself: an Ingress controller must be installed to implement it, such as NGINX Ingress, Traefik, HAProxy, or cloud controllers like the AWS Load Balancer Controller and GKE Ingress. Controllers add features such as rate limiting, authentication, redirects, and WAF integration through annotations. The newer Gateway API is the more expressive, role-oriented successor to Ingress, supporting traffic splitting and more protocols.",
    "One Ingress with a single cloud load balancer routes traffic for 15 microservices by host and path, each with an automatically renewed TLS certificate.",
    "Ingress is Layer 7 HTTP routing and needs a controller; a Service of type LoadBalancer is simpler Layer 4 exposure."
  ),
  'k8-6': n(
    "ConfigMaps store non-sensitive configuration (feature settings, URLs, configuration files) separately from container images, so the same image can run in dev, staging, and production with different settings. Secrets store sensitive values such as passwords, API tokens, and TLS keys. Both can be injected into Pods as environment variables or mounted as files; mounted files can update without restarting the Pod.\n\nKubernetes Secrets are only base64-encoded by default, not encrypted. Anyone who can read them through the API, or read etcd, can decode them. Secure them by enabling encryption at rest for etcd (ideally with a KMS provider), restricting access with RBAC, avoiding environment variables for highly sensitive data where practical, and preferably using external secret managers (AWS Secrets Manager, Vault, Azure Key Vault) via the External Secrets Operator or the CSI Secrets Store driver, with rotation and auditing.",
    "A database password lives in AWS Secrets Manager; the External Secrets Operator syncs it into a Kubernetes Secret, and rotation updates it automatically.",
    "Base64 is encoding, not encryption. Never commit Secret manifests with real values to Git unless they are encrypted (for example with SOPS or Sealed Secrets)."
  ),
  'k8-7': n(
    "Kubernetes health probes tell the kubelet about a container's state. A liveness probe checks whether the container is still healthy; if it fails repeatedly, Kubernetes restarts the container (useful for deadlocks and hung processes). A readiness probe checks whether the container can serve traffic now; while it fails, the Pod is removed from Service endpoints but not restarted (useful during warm-up, dependency outages, or overload).\n\nA startup probe gives slow-starting applications time to boot: liveness and readiness checks begin only after it succeeds, preventing premature restarts. Probes can be HTTP GET requests, TCP socket checks, gRPC checks, or commands (exec). Tune initialDelaySeconds, periodSeconds, timeoutSeconds, and failureThreshold carefully: an aggressive liveness probe that fails under load can cause restart loops that worsen outages.",
    "A Java service needs 60 seconds to start; a startup probe prevents the liveness probe from killing it during boot, while readiness keeps traffic away until it is warm.",
    "Liveness probes should not check external dependencies, or one database blip restarts every Pod at once."
  ),
  'k8-8': n(
    "Containers' file systems are ephemeral, so Kubernetes provides volumes for data that must be shared or must outlive a container. emptyDir is scratch space that lasts as long as the Pod, shared between its containers. For durable data, a PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC) requests storage of a given size and access mode (ReadWriteOnce, ReadOnlyMany, ReadWriteMany), and Kubernetes binds it to a PersistentVolume (PV).\n\nStorageClasses enable dynamic provisioning: a PVC referencing a StorageClass automatically creates the underlying disk through a CSI driver, such as AWS EBS, Azure Disk, or Google Persistent Disk for block storage, or EFS, Azure Files, and NFS for shared file storage. StorageClasses define disk type, performance, reclaim policy (Delete or Retain), and volume expansion. StatefulSets use volumeClaimTemplates to give each replica its own persistent volume.",
    "A PostgreSQL StatefulSet gets a 100 GB gp3 EBS volume per replica through a StorageClass; when a Pod moves nodes, its volume reattaches and the data persists.",
    "Set the reclaim policy to Retain for important data. The Delete default removes the disk when the PVC is deleted."
  ),
  'k8-9': n(
    "Helm is the package manager for Kubernetes. A chart bundles templated Kubernetes manifests with a values.yaml file of defaults; installing a chart renders the templates with your values and creates a release that Helm tracks, so you can upgrade, roll back (helm rollback), or uninstall it as a unit. Public charts package common software (ingress controllers, Prometheus, databases), and teams write internal charts for their own services.\n\nUse per-environment values files (values-dev.yaml, values-prod.yaml) instead of copying manifests, pin chart and image versions in version control, review rendered output (helm template or helm diff) before applying, and scan charts for insecure defaults. Alternatives and complements include Kustomize (overlays without templating) and GitOps tools such as Argo CD and Flux, which can deploy Helm charts declaratively.",
    "The platform team installs ingress-nginx, cert-manager, and kube-prometheus-stack from pinned Helm chart versions defined in a GitOps repository.",
    "Review third-party chart defaults. Many enable features, privileges, or exposure you do not want in production."
  ),
  'k8-10': n(
    "Kubernetes observability covers the cluster itself and the workloads on it. Metrics come from node_exporter (host resources), cAdvisor via the kubelet (per-container CPU, memory, and network), kube-state-metrics (the state of Kubernetes objects, such as desired versus available replicas, Pod restarts, and pending Pods), the control-plane components, and application metrics, typically scraped by Prometheus (often through the kube-prometheus-stack) and visualised in Grafana.\n\nLogs are written by containers to stdout and stderr and collected from nodes by agents such as Fluent Bit, Vector, or the OpenTelemetry Collector, enriched with Pod and namespace metadata and shipped to a central store. Kubernetes Events record scheduler and controller decisions (failed scheduling, image pull errors, OOMKilled), which are invaluable for troubleshooting. OpenTelemetry provides tracing inside clusters, and audit logs record who did what through the API.",
    "An alert on high Pod restarts plus a Kubernetes Event showing 'OOMKilled' tells the team the new release needs a higher memory limit, or has a leak.",
    "Collect Kubernetes Events and API audit logs centrally. They disappear from the cluster quickly and are key forensic evidence."
  ),

  // ---------- CI/CD & GitOps ----------
  'cicd-1': n(
    "Continuous Integration means every developer integrates small changes into the main branch frequently, with each commit or pull request automatically built and tested. The goal is to keep main always releasable and to catch integration problems within minutes.\n\nPractices that make CI work: trunk-based development or short-lived branches (hours to a couple of days), feature flags to merge incomplete features safely, a fast pipeline (lint, unit tests, static and security analysis, then build), a rule that the build is never left broken, and required status checks before merging. Pre-commit hooks catch simple issues even earlier. Measuring build duration and flakiness keeps the pipeline healthy as the codebase grows.",
    "Twenty engineers merge into main dozens of times a day; each merge runs a 7-minute pipeline, and a failure notifies the author immediately so it is fixed before others are blocked.",
    "Long-lived branches are the enemy of CI. The longer a branch lives, the more painful and risky the merge."
  ),
  'cicd-2': n(
    "After CI proves a change works, Continuous Delivery automates the rest of the release process: building a versioned artifact, running security scans, deploying to staging, running smoke, integration, and acceptance tests, and making the change deployable to production at the push of a button (often with a required approval). Continuous Deployment removes the final manual step, so every change that passes all automated checks goes to production automatically.\n\nBoth require high confidence in automated testing, repeatable infrastructure, safe release strategies, and strong observability with automated rollback. Which to choose depends on risk tolerance, regulatory requirements, and test maturity; many teams continuously deploy to lower environments and use a manual gate for production.",
    "A SaaS team continuously deploys to staging on every merge; production releases happen via one-click approval several times a day after automated tests pass.",
    "Regulated environments can still do continuous delivery. The approval step can be automated evidence plus a recorded sign-off."
  ),
  'cicd-3': n(
    "A pipeline codifies every step from commit to production. Typical stages include source checkout and dependency restore; linting and formatting; unit tests; static application security testing (SAST) and secret scanning; build; integration tests; packaging into an immutable artifact such as a container image; software composition analysis and image vulnerability scanning; SBOM generation and artifact signing; deployment to staging; smoke and end-to-end tests; approval if required; production deployment with a progressive strategy; and post-deploy verification.\n\nEach stage is a gate: a failure stops promotion and notifies the team. Run fast checks first for quick feedback, parallelise independent jobs, cache dependencies, and keep pipeline definitions in version control. Protect the pipeline itself: least-privilege credentials, pinned third-party actions, and isolated runners.",
    "A pipeline fails at the image-scan stage because a base image contains a critical OpenSSL CVE; updating the base image fixes it before staging deployment.",
    "Pin third-party CI actions and plugins to specific versions or commit SHAs. Compromised actions are a real supply-chain attack vector."
  ),
  'cicd-6': n(
    "Release strategies reduce the risk of shipping changes. Rolling updates replace instances gradually (the Kubernetes default): simple and with no extra capacity needed, but old and new versions run side by side during the rollout. Blue/green deployment runs two full environments: the new version (green) is deployed and tested alongside the current one (blue), then traffic switches over at once, and switching back is instant.\n\nCanary releases send a small percentage of real traffic (for example 1%, then 10%, then 50%) to the new version while comparing its error rates and latency with the stable version, promoting or rolling back automatically. Feature flags separate deploying code from releasing features: code ships dark and is turned on for specific users, percentages, or regions, and can be switched off instantly without a redeploy. Choose based on risk, cost, and observability maturity.",
    "A new recommendation algorithm ships behind a feature flag, is enabled for 5% of users, shows a conversion drop, and is switched off in seconds without any deployment.",
    "Database schema changes need backward-compatible migrations (expand, then contract) so old and new versions can run at the same time."
  ),
  'cicd-7': n(
    "CI/CD pipelines need credentials to push images, deploy to cloud accounts, and access registries, which makes them high-value targets. Never hard-code secrets in pipeline files or repositories, and never print them in logs. Prefer short-lived credentials: OIDC federation lets GitHub Actions, GitLab, and other CI systems exchange a signed identity token for temporary cloud credentials (for example an AWS IAM role) scoped to a specific repository and branch, so there are no long-lived keys to leak.\n\nWhere static secrets are unavoidable, store them in the CI platform's encrypted secret store or a vault (HashiCorp Vault, cloud secret managers), scope them to specific environments with protected branches and required approvals, rotate them regularly, and audit access. Grant the pipeline least privilege, separate deployment identities per environment, and run secret scanning to catch accidental commits.",
    "A GitHub Actions workflow assumes an AWS role through OIDC that only works from the main branch of one repository; no AWS access keys exist anywhere in CI.",
    "Pull requests from forks must not have access to deployment secrets. Configure CI so untrusted code cannot read them."
  ),
  'cicd-8': n(
    "Artifact management stores the outputs of builds, such as container images, language packages (npm, PyPI, Maven), Helm charts, and binaries, in registries and repositories such as Amazon ECR, Azure Container Registry, Google Artifact Registry, GitHub Packages, JFrog Artifactory, Sonatype Nexus, or Harbor.\n\nGood practice: tag artifacts with immutable, meaningful versions (semantic versions plus the Git commit SHA), enable tag immutability, deploy by content digest in production, and never rely on the floating latest tag. Scan artifacts for vulnerabilities when built and continuously afterwards, generate SBOMs, sign artifacts (Sigstore/cosign) and verify signatures at deployment, apply retention policies to control storage, and proxy public registries through a controlled internal cache to reduce supply-chain risk.",
    "An admission policy in production only allows images from the company registry that are signed by the CI system and have no critical vulnerabilities.",
    "Promote the exact same signed artifact through every environment. Never rebuild for production."
  ),
  'cicd-9': n(
    "An effective testing strategy balances confidence, speed, and cost. Unit tests validate small pieces of logic in isolation, and they are fast and numerous. Integration tests validate how components work together with real dependencies (databases, message queues), often using containers (Testcontainers). Contract tests (for example Pact) verify that a service provider still meets the expectations of its consumers, catching breaking API changes without full end-to-end environments.\n\nEnd-to-end tests validate critical user journeys through the whole system, but they are slow and brittle, so keep them few. Performance and load tests (k6, JMeter, Locust) catch regressions under realistic load. Security tests include SAST, DAST, dependency scanning, and IaC scanning. Not every test type needs to cover everything: concentrate effort on business-critical and high-risk paths, and track flakiness.",
    "Contract tests catch that the orders service renamed a field the billing service depends on, before either is deployed.",
    "Test in proportion to risk. Checkout and authentication deserve far more coverage than an admin report page."
  ),
  'cicd-10': n(
    "Release observability measures both delivery performance and the health of each change. The four DORA metrics are deployment frequency, lead time for changes (commit to production), change failure rate (the percentage of deployments causing a failure in production), and time to restore service (MTTR). Research shows elite teams deploy frequently and have low failure rates at the same time; speed and stability reinforce each other.\n\nFor each release, monitor error rates, latency percentiles, saturation, and key business metrics, compared with the previous version and with deployment markers on dashboards. Automated analysis (for example Argo Rollouts or Flagger checking Prometheus metrics during a canary) can promote a release when metrics stay healthy and roll back automatically when they degrade, which dramatically reduces MTTR for bad deployments.",
    "A canary's error rate rises to 2% against 0.1% for the stable version; automated analysis aborts the rollout and routes all traffic back within 3 minutes.",
    "Track the DORA metrics over time. They show whether process changes actually improve delivery."
  ),

  // ---------- Kubernetes & GitOps (expert) ----------
  'k2-1': n(
    "The Kubernetes control plane manages the cluster's desired and actual state. The kube-apiserver is the front door: every request from kubectl, controllers, kubelets, and operators goes through it, where it is authenticated, authorised with RBAC, validated, passed through admission controllers, and persisted. The scheduler assigns new Pods to nodes based on resource requests, affinity and anti-affinity rules, taints and tolerations, and topology constraints.\n\nThe controller manager runs reconciliation loops (Deployment, ReplicaSet, Node, Job, and other controllers) that continually drive actual state towards desired state. The cloud controller manager integrates cloud resources such as load balancers and routes. etcd stores all cluster data. Managed services (EKS, AKS, GKE) run the control plane for you, but you still control access to it. Whoever controls the API server controls the cluster, so restrict network access, use strong authentication, and minimise cluster-admin rights.",
    "A misconfigured RBAC binding gives a CI service account cluster-admin; an attacker who compromises the pipeline can create privileged Pods and take over every node.",
    "Keep the API server endpoint private or IP-restricted, and audit every cluster-admin binding."
  ),
  'k2-2': n(
    "Custom Resource Definitions extend the Kubernetes API with new object types, such as PostgresCluster, Certificate, KafkaTopic, or Rollout, that behave like built-in resources: you create them with YAML, and kubectl, RBAC, admission control, and GitOps tools all work with them. On their own, CRDs only store data; a controller (often part of an operator) watches the custom resources and acts on them.\n\nCRDs let platform teams offer higher-level, domain-specific abstractions: developers declare 'a Postgres database with 3 replicas and daily backups' instead of wiring together StatefulSets, Services, and CronJobs. Treat CRDs as APIs: define schemas with validation (OpenAPI v3), version them (v1alpha1, v1beta1, v1) with conversion webhooks when they change, document them, and govern who can create or modify both the definitions and the instances.",
    "cert-manager's Certificate CRD lets a team request a TLS certificate declaratively; its controller obtains and renews it automatically from Let's Encrypt.",
    "Installing a CRD and its controller extends your cluster's attack surface. Review what permissions the controller needs."
  ),
  'k2-3': n(
    "The operator pattern packages human operational knowledge into software. An operator is a controller that watches custom resources and automates the full lifecycle of a complex, often stateful application: provisioning, configuration, scaling, backups and restores, upgrades, failover, and self-healing, continuously reconciling the application to the declared desired state.\n\nExamples include the Prometheus Operator, database operators (CloudNativePG, Zalando Postgres, Percona), Strimzi for Kafka, Elastic Cloud on Kubernetes, and cert-manager. Frameworks such as Kubebuilder, the Operator SDK, and kopf (Python) help build them. Operators replace runbooks for routine operations, but they need careful RBAC (they often hold broad permissions), testing of upgrade paths, and monitoring of the operator itself.",
    "A CloudNativePG operator detects that the primary database Pod failed, promotes a replica, and repoints the service in seconds, without a DBA being paged.",
    "Evaluate an operator's maturity (the capability levels, from basic install to autopilot) before trusting it with production data."
  ),
  'k2-4': n(
    "Progressive delivery reduces release risk by exposing new versions to gradually increasing traffic or users, while automatically evaluating their health. Techniques include canary releases (shifting traffic in steps such as 5%, 25%, 50%, and 100%), blue/green with automated verification, A/B testing, and feature flags for user-level control.\n\nIn Kubernetes, Argo Rollouts and Flagger implement this, using service meshes, ingress controllers, or the Gateway API to split traffic and querying metrics providers (Prometheus, Datadog, CloudWatch) against success criteria based on SLOs, such as error rate below 1% and p99 latency below 500 ms. Releases are promoted automatically when healthy and rolled back automatically when not, turning deployment from a risky event into a routine, low-stress operation.",
    "Argo Rollouts shifts 10% of traffic to v2, runs a 10-minute analysis against Prometheus error-rate queries, then continues to 50% and 100% only if the thresholds hold.",
    "Automated analysis is only as good as its metrics. Include business signals (such as orders per minute), not just HTTP status codes."
  ),
  'k2-5': n(
    "A GitOps architecture typically separates application source repositories from environment configuration repositories, which hold the Kubernetes manifests, Helm values, or Kustomize overlays for each cluster and environment. CI builds and tests code, publishes a signed image, and then updates the image reference in the config repository through a pull request, or automatically via image automation.\n\nA GitOps controller running inside each cluster (Argo CD or Flux) pulls the config repository, compares it with the live cluster state, and reconciles differences, reporting sync and health status. Patterns include app-of-apps or ApplicationSets for managing many applications and clusters, sync waves for ordering, and automated drift correction. Because the controller pulls changes, CI never needs cluster credentials, and git revert provides instant, audited rollback.",
    "An ApplicationSet deploys the same service to 40 regional clusters from one Git repository; promoting to production is a reviewed pull request that changes one values file.",
    "Keep secrets out of plain Git. Use SOPS, Sealed Secrets, or an external secrets operator with GitOps."
  ),
  'k2-6': n(
    "Admission control is the Kubernetes API server's final checkpoint before an object is stored. Validating admission webhooks can reject non-compliant requests, and mutating webhooks can modify them (for example injecting sidecars or default labels). Policy engines, namely OPA Gatekeeper (with Rego policies), Kyverno (policies written in YAML), and built-in ValidatingAdmissionPolicy (using CEL expressions), use this mechanism to enforce guardrails.\n\nTypical policies include requiring resource requests and limits, mandatory labels, allowing images only from approved registries (with signature verification), blocking privileged containers, host networking, and hostPath mounts, enforcing runAsNonRoot and read-only root file systems, and restricting Ingress host names. Pod Security Admission provides built-in baseline and restricted profiles. Enforcing at deploy time shifts governance left from after-the-fact audits, and running policies in audit mode first avoids breaking teams.",
    "Kyverno rejects a Deployment that runs a privileged container from Docker Hub, returning a clear message explaining which policy failed and how to fix it.",
    "Roll out new policies in audit or warn mode first, fix violations, then switch to enforce."
  ),
  'k2-7': n(
    "Multi-tenancy runs workloads for many teams, applications, or customers on shared clusters while keeping them isolated. Soft multi-tenancy, for trusted internal teams, typically uses a namespace per tenant with RBAC limiting each team to its namespaces, ResourceQuotas and LimitRanges to cap and default CPU, memory, and object counts, NetworkPolicies to block cross-namespace traffic by default, and Pod Security standards.\n\nHard multi-tenancy, for untrusted tenants such as different customers, needs stronger boundaries: dedicated node pools with taints and tolerations, sandboxed runtimes (gVisor, Kata Containers), virtual clusters (vCluster), or separate clusters per tenant. The three pillars are identity and access isolation, resource isolation (quotas and fair scheduling), and network isolation. Cost, operational overhead, and the level of trust between tenants determine the right model.",
    "Each product team gets its own namespace with a quota of 20 CPUs, default-deny network policies, and RBAC to manage only its own resources.",
    "Namespaces are not a hard security boundary. Untrusted tenants need node, runtime, or cluster-level isolation."
  ),
  'k2-8': n(
    "etcd is the distributed, strongly consistent key-value store that holds all Kubernetes cluster data: every object, secret, and status. It uses the Raft consensus algorithm, so a cluster of 3 or 5 members stays available as long as a majority (quorum) survives; losing quorum makes the cluster read-only and unmanageable, although existing workloads keep running.\n\netcd performance directly affects the whole control plane: slow disks (high fsync latency) or network latency between members slow down every API request and can cause leader elections. Operate it with fast SSDs, low-latency networking, regular compaction and defragmentation, and monitoring of leader changes, database size, and request latency. Take frequent snapshots (etcdctl snapshot save) and practise restores, because an unrecoverable etcd means rebuilding the cluster. Encrypt it at rest, and restrict access to its endpoints and client certificates, since it contains every Secret.",
    "A self-managed cluster's etcd disk fills up; API writes fail until the team compacts and defragments it. Monitoring on database size would have warned them days earlier.",
    "Test etcd restores regularly. A backup you have never restored is only a hope."
  ),
  'k2-9': n(
    "A service mesh adds a dedicated infrastructure layer for service-to-service communication. A data plane of proxies, either Envoy sidecars injected into each Pod or node-level and eBPF-based proxies in 'ambient' or sidecarless modes, intercepts traffic and applies policy set by a control plane. It provides, without application code changes, automatic mutual TLS (encryption plus workload identity), fine-grained authorisation between services, retries, timeouts and circuit breaking, traffic splitting for canaries, and consistent metrics and traces for every call.\n\nPopular meshes include Istio, Linkerd, Consul, and Cilium's service mesh. The costs are operational complexity, added latency and resource use from proxies, and a steeper learning curve. Adopt a mesh when cross-cutting needs such as zero-trust mTLS, traffic management, and uniform observability across many services outgrow what libraries and ingress can handle cleanly.",
    "Enabling Istio strict mTLS encrypts all traffic between 200 microservices and allows the payments service to accept calls only from the checkout service's identity.",
    "Start with the features you need, typically mTLS and observability, and add advanced traffic management later."
  ),
  'k2-10': n(
    "Cluster API (CAPI) is a Kubernetes sub-project that manages Kubernetes clusters declaratively using Kubernetes itself. A management cluster runs CAPI controllers, and the workload clusters you want are described as custom resources (Cluster, MachineDeployment, and control-plane objects). Infrastructure providers implement those resources on AWS, Azure, Google Cloud, vSphere, bare metal, and others, creating the machines, networking, and control planes.\n\nThis brings the Deployment model to whole clusters: create them from templates, scale node groups, perform rolling Kubernetes version upgrades by changing a field, and replace unhealthy machines automatically with MachineHealthChecks. Combined with GitOps, cluster definitions live in Git, so entire fleets of clusters are version-controlled, reviewable, and reproducible, which is ideal for platform teams managing many clusters across environments and regions.",
    "A platform team upgrades 25 clusters from Kubernetes 1.30 to 1.31 by changing the version in Git; Cluster API rolls new control-plane and worker machines cluster by cluster.",
    "Protect the management cluster carefully. It holds credentials to create and destroy every workload cluster."
  ),
  'kg-8': n(
    "StatefulSets manage stateful applications that need stable identities and persistent storage, such as databases (PostgreSQL, MySQL, MongoDB), message brokers (Kafka, RabbitMQ), and coordination services (ZooKeeper, etcd). Unlike Deployments, each Pod gets a stable, ordinal name (kafka-0, kafka-1, kafka-2) and a stable DNS name through a headless Service, which it keeps across rescheduling.\n\nvolumeClaimTemplates give each replica its own PersistentVolumeClaim, which stays bound to that ordinal even if the Pod moves to another node. Pods are created, scaled, and updated in order (by default one at a time, from lowest to highest ordinal on creation and highest to lowest on termination), which respects clustering requirements. Running stateful services on Kubernetes still requires careful backup, restore, and upgrade procedures, which is why operators are often used on top of StatefulSets.",
    "A 3-node Kafka cluster runs as a StatefulSet; kafka-1 is rescheduled after a node failure, keeps its name and its disk, and rejoins the cluster with its data intact.",
    "Deleting a StatefulSet does not delete its PVCs by default, which protects data but can leave orphaned volumes and costs."
  ),
  'kg-9': n(
    "Organisations often run many Kubernetes clusters: per environment, per region for latency and resilience, per compliance boundary, per cloud, or at the edge. Multi-cluster management coordinates workloads, configuration, identity, and policy across them. Approaches include GitOps fleet tools (Argo CD ApplicationSets, Flux, Rancher Fleet), control-plane federation projects (such as Karmada and the older KubeFed), Crossplane for managing cloud infrastructure and clusters declaratively, and multi-cluster service meshes and networking (Istio multi-cluster, Cilium Cluster Mesh) for cross-cluster service discovery and failover.\n\nChallenges include keeping configuration and policies consistent, cross-cluster networking and latency, data locality and replication, split-brain risks during partitions, unified observability, and consistent identity and access control across clusters.",
    "An online game runs clusters in five regions; GitOps deploys identical services to all of them, and global DNS routes players to the nearest healthy region.",
    "Prefer independent clusters with shared GitOps and policy over tightly coupled federation. Simpler failure domains are easier to reason about."
  ),

  // ---------- Site Reliability Engineering ----------
  'sre-1': n(
    "Site Reliability Engineering, created at Google, treats operations as a software engineering problem. SRE teams apply engineering to reliability: defining SLOs from the user's perspective, using error budgets to balance feature velocity against stability, automating away repetitive operational work (toil), building monitoring and alerting around user-visible symptoms, managing incidents methodically, and running blameless postmortems.\n\nA common guideline caps toil at about 50% of an SRE's time, with the rest spent on engineering projects that improve reliability and scalability, so systems can grow without operations headcount growing linearly. SRE also emphasises capacity planning, change management (most outages follow changes), simplicity, and shared ownership of production between developers and SREs. The Google SRE books, including the free Site Reliability Engineering and The Site Reliability Workbook, are the classic references.",
    "An SRE team spends a quarter automating certificate renewal and database failover, eliminating two of the most common causes of pages.",
    "SRE is not 'ops with a new name'. Its hallmark is engineering work that removes operational burden and manages reliability with data."
  ),
  'sre-2': n(
    "An error budget is the amount of unreliability a service is allowed within its SLO window: error budget = 100% − SLO. With a 99.9% monthly availability SLO, the budget is 0.1%, about 43 minutes of full downtime or the equivalent share of failed requests. Budgets turn reliability into a measurable resource that product and engineering share.\n\nWhile budget remains, teams can release freely and take calculated risks. An agreed error budget policy defines what happens when it is exhausted or burning too fast: for example freezing risky feature releases, requiring extra review, and redirecting engineering effort to reliability work until the service recovers. This replaces subjective arguments ('we need more stability' versus 'we need to ship') with data, and it discourages over-investing in reliability users do not need.",
    "After two incidents consume the quarter's error budget, the product team agrees to pause launches for two weeks while engineers fix the underlying database failover issue.",
    "Get product leadership to sign the error budget policy in advance; enforcing it during an incident without agreement rarely works."
  ),
  'sre-3': n(
    "Toil is operational work that is manual, repetitive, automatable, tactical (reactive rather than strategic), without lasting value, and that grows linearly with service size: manually restarting services, running the same fix for recurring alerts, provisioning accounts by hand, copying data, rotating certificates manually, or processing routine tickets.\n\nSRE measures toil (through surveys, ticket analysis, and time tracking) and deliberately reduces it through automation, self-service tools, better system design, and eliminating the root causes of recurring issues. A common target keeps toil below 50% of the team's time. Some toil is unavoidable, but unchecked toil crowds out improvement work, burns people out, and does not scale. A useful heuristic: if you have done a task manually three times, consider automating it.",
    "An SRE team replaces manual weekly database user provisioning (about 6 hours a week) with a self-service workflow and approval, freeing time for reliability projects.",
    "Measure toil before and after automation to prove value and prioritise the next target."
  ),
  'sre-4': n(
    "Incident management provides a structured, calm way to restore service during significant outages. Incidents are detected through SLO-based alerts, monitoring, or customer reports, then declared and assigned a severity. Clear roles prevent chaos: the incident commander coordinates and makes decisions but does not debug hands-on; the communications lead updates stakeholders and status pages; the operations or technical lead directs the investigation; and subject-matter experts investigate and act. Many organisations use frameworks based on the Incident Command System.\n\nThe priority is mitigation, restoring service quickly by rollback, failover, feature flag, or scaling, before root cause analysis. Maintain a timeline in a dedicated channel, communicate at regular intervals, and hand over explicitly between shifts. Every significant incident ends with a blameless postmortem that produces tracked action items.",
    "During a regional outage, the incident commander assigns one engineer to failover, one to investigate, and the communications lead posts status updates every 20 minutes.",
    "The incident commander should not also be the person debugging. Coordination is a full-time job during major incidents."
  ),
  'sre-5': n(
    "A blameless postmortem is a written review after an incident that focuses on how the system, processes, and tools allowed the failure, not on who made a mistake. It assumes everyone acted reasonably with the information they had. That psychological safety encourages honest, detailed accounts, which surface the real contributing factors.\n\nA good postmortem includes a summary, impact (users affected, duration, SLO and error budget consumed), a detailed timeline, root cause and contributing factors, what went well, what went poorly, where the team got lucky, and specific action items with owners, priorities, and due dates. Postmortems are shared widely so other teams learn, and action items are tracked to completion. Organisations that blame individuals learn less and see incidents repeat.",
    "A postmortem for a bad config push identifies missing validation in the deploy tool and no canary stage; both are fixed, and the same failure cannot recur.",
    "Replace 'human error' as a root cause with the question: why did the system make that error easy and its impact large?"
  ),
  'sre-6': n(
    "Capacity planning ensures a service has enough resources to meet demand and its SLOs, including peak periods and failure scenarios, without wasteful over-provisioning. Inputs include organic growth trends, seasonality (holidays, month-end, sporting events), planned launches and marketing campaigns, and constraints in dependencies (database connections, third-party API quotas, cloud service limits).\n\nPlan for peak load plus headroom, and for N+1 or N+2 redundancy: the system must still handle peak load after losing a node or an entire Availability Zone. Validate assumptions with load testing and track utilisation against thresholds. In the cloud, auto scaling covers much short-term variation, but you still need to plan quotas, reserved capacity, database scaling, and cost. Poor capacity planning leads to throttling, saturation, and outages at the worst moments.",
    "Before Black Friday, load tests reveal the database connection pool saturates at 3x normal traffic; the team adds read replicas and connection pooling weeks in advance.",
    "Check cloud service quotas (instance limits, IP addresses, API rate limits) before peak events. Hitting a quota blocks auto scaling."
  ),
  'sre-7': n(
    "Changes, meaning code deployments, configuration pushes, infrastructure updates, and feature flag flips, are the single largest cause of production incidents. SRE-style change management reduces this risk without slowing delivery to a crawl: make changes small and frequent, automate them through pipelines, roll them out progressively (canaries and staged rollouts across zones and regions), gate them on automated health checks, and ensure fast, tested rollback.\n\nOther practices include change freezes or extra scrutiny during high-risk periods, preventing simultaneous risky changes, peer review for configuration changes as well as code, and correlating changes with anomalies by overlaying deployment and config-change markers on dashboards, so responders can immediately ask 'what changed?'",
    "A global configuration change rolls out to one region first, and automated checks detect elevated errors and halt it before the other nine regions are affected.",
    "Treat configuration changes like code: reviewed, tested, and rolled out progressively. Many major outages were 'just a config change'."
  ),
  'sre-8': n(
    "Alerting hygiene keeps paging trustworthy and on-call sustainable. Page only when a human must act now to prevent or reduce user impact; route everything else to tickets or chat. Base alerts on symptoms that users feel (SLO burn rate, error rates, latency) rather than every internal cause. Every alert needs a clear severity, an owner, and a runbook link.\n\nReduce noise by requiring conditions to persist, deduplicating and grouping related alerts, suppressing alerts during maintenance, and deleting alerts that never lead to action. Track pages per on-call shift, off-hours pages, mean time to acknowledge (MTTA), mean time to resolve (MTTR), and alert precision. Review alerts regularly as a team. Protect on-call engineers from alert storms with routing, escalation policies, and sustainable rotation sizes.",
    "A quarterly alert review deletes 30 alerts that never required action and converts 12 into tickets, halving night pages without missing any real incidents.",
    "If a page can safely wait until morning, it should not be a page."
  ),
  'sre-9': n(
    "Reliability is ultimately about whether users can accomplish what they came to do: log in, search, pay, stream. Backend health checks can show all green while users suffer from broken JavaScript, CDN misconfiguration, DNS problems, mobile app crashes, third-party script failures, or regional network issues.\n\nMeasure user-facing reliability as close to the user as possible: SLIs defined on critical user journeys, real user monitoring (RUM) and client-side error and performance metrics (such as Core Web Vitals), synthetic probes that exercise key flows from real user locations, and edge or load-balancer logs instead of only application server logs. Include dependencies users rely on, such as payment providers and authentication, in the picture. Prioritise reliability work by user impact.",
    "Server dashboards are green, but RUM shows checkout failing for Safari users after a front-end release; a synthetic browser test is added to catch this in future.",
    "Define SLOs per critical user journey (such as 'checkout completes') rather than per server."
  ),
  'sre-10': n(
    "SRE culture aligns development and operations around shared ownership of production and shared incentives for reliability. Developers participate in on-call for the services they build ('you build it, you run it'), often with SRE support; production readiness reviews happen before launch; and reliability work (automation, capacity, resilience) is planned and valued alongside features.\n\nHealthy culture is blameless, treating incidents as learning opportunities; data-driven, using SLOs and error budgets; and sustainable, with reasonable on-call load, compensation, and no reliance on individual heroics. Career paths should reward engineering that prevents outages (automation, design reviews, tooling) as much as dramatic firefighting. Knowledge sharing through postmortem reviews, runbooks, and training spreads reliability expertise across the organisation.",
    "A company adds reliability objectives to every product team's quarterly goals and recognises engineers who eliminate recurring incidents, not just those who fix outages at 3 a.m.",
    "Rewarding heroics encourages fragile systems. Reward the work that makes heroics unnecessary."
  ),
  'site-reliability-engineering-c11': n(
    "SLIs, SLOs, and SLAs form the measurement backbone of SRE. An SLI (Service Level Indicator) is a metric of good behaviour, usually a ratio of good events to valid events: successful requests over total requests, requests faster than 300 ms over total, or fresh records over total. An SLO (Service Level Objective) is the target for that SLI over a rolling window, such as 99.9% over 28 days. An SLA (Service Level Agreement) is an external, contractual promise with consequences (service credits or penalties), deliberately set looser than the internal SLO so there is room to react.\n\nChoose a few SLIs per service tied to critical user journeys, set SLOs based on what users actually need and what the system can realistically achieve rather than maximum possible reliability, publish them, review them regularly, and use missed or burning SLOs to trigger error budget decisions.",
    "A search API defines two SLIs, availability (non-5xx responses) and latency (p95 under 400 ms), each with a 99.5% SLO, and an SLA of 99% in customer contracts.",
    "If every metric is an SLO, nothing is. Keep a small number of SLOs that genuinely reflect user happiness."
  ),
  'site-reliability-engineering-c14': n(
    "Reliability testing proactively exercises the failure modes and load conditions a system will eventually face in production. Load and stress testing (k6, Locust, Gatling, JMeter) validates capacity and finds breaking points and bottlenecks. Soak tests run sustained load for hours to reveal memory leaks and resource exhaustion. Chaos experiments inject failures such as instance termination, zone outages, dependency latency and errors, network partitions, and resource saturation to confirm that redundancy, timeouts, retries, circuit breakers, and failover actually work.\n\nDisaster recovery tests restore backups and fail over whole regions to prove that RTO and RPO targets can be met. Game days rehearse realistic incidents end to end, testing people, runbooks, alerts, and communication as well as technology. Regular testing uncovers hidden dependencies and technical debt before real outages do.",
    "A game day simulating the loss of the primary region reveals that the DR runbook references a decommissioned server; it is fixed months before a real regional outage.",
    "An untested failover or backup should be treated as not working until proven otherwise."
  ),

  // ---------- Infrastructure as Code Deep Dive ----------
  'iac-1': n(
    "Declarative IaC describes the desired end state (for example 'three subnets, a load balancer, and an encrypted database of this size') and lets the tool work out how to get there: which resources to create, update in place, replace, or delete. Terraform and OpenTofu, CloudFormation, Bicep and ARM, Pulumi (declarative state described with general-purpose languages), and Kubernetes manifests all work this way.\n\nThe engine compares the desired configuration with the current real state and computes a plan, making changes idempotent (applying the same code twice changes nothing the second time) and predictable. By contrast, imperative scripts (a series of CLI commands) describe steps, break when the starting state differs, are hard to review, and cannot easily detect drift, so they are not true IaC even if stored in Git. Declarative definitions become the single source of truth, reviewed as diffs in pull requests.",
    "Changing `instance_count = 3` to `5` in Terraform makes the plan add exactly two instances; applying the same code again reports 'no changes'.",
    "Idempotency is the key property: re-running declarative IaC should be safe and converge on the same result."
  ),
  'iac-2': n(
    "Terraform's core workflow is init, plan, and apply. terraform init downloads providers and modules and configures the state backend. terraform plan refreshes knowledge of the real infrastructure, compares it with the configuration and state, and produces an execution plan showing each resource to be created (+), updated in place (~), replaced (-/+, destroy and recreate), or destroyed (-), including which attribute changes force replacement.\n\nHumans, or automated policy checks, review the plan before terraform apply executes it. In CI/CD, the plan is saved as an artifact (terraform plan -out=tfplan), posted to the pull request for review, and exactly that saved plan is applied after approval, so what was reviewed is what runs. Watch for unexpected replacements or destroys of stateful resources, protect critical resources with lifecycle { prevent_destroy = true }, and never run apply blindly against production.",
    "A plan unexpectedly shows `-/+` for the production database because a renamed parameter forces replacement; the reviewer catches it and the change is reworked to avoid data loss.",
    "Read every 'must be replaced' and 'destroy' line in a plan. Those are where data loss happens."
  ),
  'iac-3': n(
    "Terraform state is a file that maps every resource in your configuration to the real object it manages (IDs, attributes, and dependencies), so Terraform knows what exists and what to change. For teams, state must be stored in a remote backend, such as S3 (with DynamoDB, or native S3 locking in newer versions), Azure Blob Storage, Google Cloud Storage, or Terraform Cloud and HCP, with locking to prevent two people from applying at once and corrupting it, versioning to recover from mistakes, and encryption at rest.\n\nState often contains sensitive values in plain text, including generated passwords, private keys, and connection strings, so restrict access tightly and treat any state leak like a credential leak. Split state by environment and component to limit the blast radius. Never hand-edit state files; use terraform state mv, rm, and import (or moved and import blocks) for deliberate changes.",
    "Two engineers run apply simultaneously without locking, corrupting state; after moving to an S3 backend with locking and versioning, the team restores the previous state version in minutes.",
    "Never commit terraform.tfstate to Git. It frequently contains secrets."
  ),
  'iac-4': n(
    "Drift is any difference between the infrastructure your code describes and what actually exists. It is caused by manual console changes ('quick fixes' during incidents), other automation or scripts, provider-side defaults changing, or resources modified by other tools. Drift makes environments inconsistent, breaks the assumption that code is the source of truth, and can hide security problems such as a security group opened by hand.\n\nDetect it by running scheduled terraform plan (or plan -refresh-only) jobs and alerting when changes appear, or with CloudFormation drift detection, Terraform Cloud drift detection, or CSPM tools. Resolve each case deliberately: either revert the manual change by re-applying the code, or, if the change was intended, update the code to match and import resources where needed. Reduce drift by limiting console write access in production and routing changes through pipelines.",
    "A nightly drift check flags that someone opened port 22 to 0.0.0.0/0 on a production security group; re-applying Terraform closes it and the incident is investigated.",
    "Restrict production write access in the console to break-glass roles, so IaC stays the only normal way to change infrastructure."
  ),
  'iac-5': n(
    "Modules package reusable infrastructure patterns, such as a standard VPC with subnets and flow logs, an encrypted S3 bucket with logging, or a database cluster with backups and monitoring, behind a small interface of input variables and outputs. Teams compose modules to build environments rather than copying hundreds of lines of resources.\n\nModules encode organisational standards (encryption on, public access off, mandatory tags) so every team gets secure defaults automatically. Distribute approved modules through a private registry (Terraform Cloud, Artifactory, a Git repository with tags), version them with semantic versioning, document their inputs and outputs, test them, and pin versions in consuming code (version = \"~> 3.2\") so upstream changes are adopted deliberately. Avoid over-abstraction: modules with dozens of options become as complex as the resources they wrap.",
    "A platform team's 'secure-bucket' module enforces encryption, versioning, and blocked public access; 60 application teams use it, so no bucket is ever created insecurely.",
    "Pin module versions in production. Referencing a moving branch means someone else's change can alter your infrastructure unexpectedly."
  ),
  'iac-6': n(
    "Environment strategy keeps development, staging, and production isolated yet consistent. Each environment should have its own state (and usually its own cloud account or subscription), so an experiment in dev can never modify production. The same modules are reused across environments with different variable values (instance sizes, replica counts, domain names), typically through per-environment directories or configuration files, Terragrunt, or workspaces (used carefully).\n\nPromote code, not state: a change is merged, applied to dev, validated, then applied to staging and production through the pipeline, with reviews and approvals at each gate. Layer variables as global defaults, then environment overrides, then secrets injected at runtime. Ephemeral environments created per pull request give realistic testing and are destroyed automatically afterwards to control cost.",
    "The same Terraform root module deploys dev with 1 small instance and prod with 6 large instances across 3 AZs, driven by separate tfvars files and separate state in separate accounts.",
    "Separate accounts or subscriptions per environment are the strongest isolation. Workspaces alone share credentials and backends."
  ),
  'iac-7': n(
    "Infrastructure code deserves tests, just like application code. Static checks run in seconds: terraform fmt and validate, linters (TFLint), and security and compliance scanners (Checkov, tfsec and Trivy, KICS) that flag issues such as unencrypted storage, public exposure, or missing logging. Plan-level tests assert on the terraform plan output, for example that no resources are destroyed or that tags are present, often with policy as code (OPA/Conftest, Sentinel).\n\nUnit-style tests (Terraform's native terraform test framework) validate module logic with mocked or real providers. Integration tests (such as Terratest in Go) deploy real infrastructure into an isolated test account, verify behaviour (the website responds, the database accepts connections, the security group blocks other ports), then destroy everything. Run these in CI on every change so infrastructure defects are caught before they reach production.",
    "A Checkov scan in CI blocks a pull request that creates an RDS instance without encryption and with public accessibility enabled.",
    "Run integration tests in a dedicated sandbox account with budgets and automatic clean-up to avoid orphaned, costly resources."
  ),
  'iac-8': n(
    "Secrets such as database passwords, API keys, and TLS private keys must never be hard-coded in IaC templates, variable files committed to Git, or pipeline logs. Instead, reference secrets stored in managed secret stores (AWS Secrets Manager and SSM Parameter Store, Azure Key Vault, Google Secret Manager, HashiCorp Vault), generate them at deploy time (for example a random password stored directly into Secrets Manager), or use dynamic, short-lived credentials from Vault.\n\nBe aware that Terraform state can still record secret values in plain text even when variables are marked sensitive, so encrypt and restrict state, and prefer designs where the secret never passes through Terraform, such as managed master passwords handled by the database service or ephemeral resources in newer Terraform versions. Mark sensitive variables and outputs, scan repositories for committed secrets, and immediately rotate any secret that ever appeared in code, state, logs, or chat.",
    "An RDS instance uses the provider-managed master password feature, so the password lives only in Secrets Manager and never appears in Terraform code or state.",
    "`sensitive = true` hides values from CLI output. It does not remove them from state."
  ),
  'iac-9': n(
    "AWS CloudFormation is AWS's native IaC service: templates in YAML or JSON (or generated by AWS CDK in TypeScript, Python, and other languages) are deployed as stacks, with state managed by AWS, automatic rollback on failure, change sets for previewing changes, drift detection, StackSets for multi-account deployment, and day-one support for many new AWS features. It only manages AWS (plus registry extensions).\n\nTerraform (and its open-source fork OpenTofu) is multi-cloud and multi-service. Thousands of providers manage AWS, Azure, Google Cloud, Kubernetes, GitHub, Datadog, Cloudflare, and more with one language (HCL) and workflow. You manage state yourself (or via Terraform Cloud and HCP) and review plans before apply. Both are declarative. Choose CloudFormation or CDK for AWS-only organisations wanting deep native integration and managed state; choose Terraform for multi-cloud, hybrid, or SaaS-heavy estates and a large module ecosystem.",
    "An AWS-only startup uses CDK for its app stacks, while a multinational manages AWS, Azure, Cloudflare DNS, and GitHub repositories from one Terraform codebase.",
    "Consistency matters more than the tool. Standardise on one primary IaC tool per organisation where possible."
  ),
  'iac-10': n(
    "Policy and governance for IaC enforce organisational standards automatically before infrastructure is created. Policy as code tools evaluate plans or templates in CI/CD: HashiCorp Sentinel in Terraform Cloud and Enterprise, Open Policy Agent (OPA) with Conftest, Checkov custom policies, AWS CloudFormation Guard and Hooks, and Azure Policy for Bicep and ARM deployments. Typical rules include mandatory tags, approved regions and instance types, encryption required, no public storage or databases, cost limits, and prohibited services.\n\nThe pipeline blocks non-compliant changes regardless of who wrote them, with clear messages explaining how to fix them. Exceptions go through a documented, time-limited approval process. Policies are versioned and tested like code, and evaluation results provide audit evidence for compliance frameworks. Complement pre-deployment checks with runtime guardrails (SCPs, Azure Policy, AWS Config) to catch anything created outside IaC.",
    "An OPA policy in the pipeline blocks a Terraform plan that would create resources outside the approved EU regions, keeping the company within its data residency rules.",
    "Combine preventive checks in CI with runtime guardrails. Not every change goes through your pipeline."
  ),
  'infrastructure-as-code-deep-dive-c15': n(
    "Immutable infrastructure means servers, containers, and other components are never modified after deployment. To change anything (patch the OS, update the application, change configuration), you build a new, versioned image and replace the old instances with new ones. Images are built by pipelines using tools such as HashiCorp Packer (VM images such as AMIs), EC2 Image Builder, or Dockerfiles (container images), tested, and then rolled out.\n\nBecause every instance comes from the same tested image, environments stay consistent, configuration drift and 'snowflake servers' disappear, rollbacks are simple (redeploy the previous image), and scaling out is fast and predictable. Immutable infrastructure pairs naturally with auto-scaling groups, blue/green and canary deployments, and containers. It also improves security: SSH access and in-place changes can be disabled entirely, and compromised instances are simply replaced.",
    "Monthly OS patching becomes 'build a new AMI with Packer, test it, and roll the auto-scaling group'; no one logs into production servers to run updates.",
    "If you must SSH in to change production servers, you are not immutable. Rebuild the image instead."
  ),
};
